<urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AY8-QA-002/ay8-2>
  a triage:Occurrence ;
  triage:file "crates/atm-herdr/tests/transport_fixture.rs" ;
  triage:line 170 ;
  triage:snippet "socket_fixture_matrix_covers_late_start_and_one_request_connections / socket_fixture_matrix_covers_no_newline_oversized_and_stalled_read (partial matrix)" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:closed false ;
  triage:branch "feature/ay8-herdr-socket-transport" ;
  triage:headSha "26ea2806a" ;
  triage:occursIn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/ay8-26ea2806a> ;
  triage:note "quality-mgr qa-pr1280-r3: FakeHerdrSocket is now wired (no longer dead code) and exercised by every_manifest_is_equivalent_over_cli_and_socket_transports plus two new fixture tests covering: absent endpoint, late-start with server accepting after client dispatch, rejection of a second request on the same one-request connection, no-newline response, oversized (>1MiB) response, and stalled-read timeout+cancellation. Still missing from the required D7 scenario matrix: Windows pipe-busy retry under paused Tokio time, 16/17-caller permit-saturation proving resource release, cancellation specifically at the permit-wait and connect stages (only write/read-stage timeout is covered), and an AY.2-style version-recording replay run through the socket fixture. Finding stays open (blocking); this is real, substantial progress, not a rubber stamp." .

<urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AY8-QA-002/ay8-3>
  a triage:Occurrence ;
  triage:file "crates/atm-herdr/tests/transport_fixture.rs" ;
  triage:line 12 ;
  triage:snippet "#[cfg(unix)]\n#[path = \"support/fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs\"]\nmod fake_herdr_socket;" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:closed false ;
  triage:branch "feature/ay8-herdr-socket-transport" ;
  triage:headSha "690f86ea5" ;
  triage:occursIn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/ay8-690f86ea5> ;
  triage:note "quality-mgr qa-pr1280-r4, ruling per explicit non-negotiable directive: the original r1 'unwired dead code' characterization is WRONG at this pin and every prior one back through 26ea2806a -- direct read confirms FakeHerdrSocket::bind/serve_once/serve_and_stall are genuinely instantiated and driven by 4 real tokio tests in transport_fixture.rs (absent endpoint, late-start, rejected second connection, no-newline, oversized response, stalled-read+timeout, plus full CLI/socket manifest equivalence). Separately, transport_socket.rs's own #[cfg(test)] unit tests (permit_wait_uses_the_call_deadline_and_releases_cleanly, seventeen_callers_are_bounded_by_sixteen_permits, cancelling_a_permit_wait_releases_all_state, all using tokio::test(start_paused = true)) independently and rigorously prove the 16-permit cap, the 17th-caller-times-out-under-deadline saturation case, and permit-wait cancellation with full state release -- so those two D7 sub-requirements are genuinely met, just not via the fake-socket fixture file. HOWEVER the finding still stands (blocking, not closed) -- precise remaining D7 gap at 690f86ea5: (1) no stalled-connect or stalled-write scenario exists anywhere, only stalled-read (serve_and_stall stalls after accepting+reading the request, before writing the response); (2) FakeHerdrSocket's Windows named-pipe implementation (fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs:61-113, cfg(windows)) is entirely dead code -- transport_fixture.rs's `mod fake_herdr_socket;` at line 12 is gated #[cfg(unix)] only, so it never compiles on the Windows CI lane, and transport_socket.rs's connect_named_pipe (ERROR_PIPE_BUSY retry loop, lines 349-371) has zero test coverage anywhere, unix or windows -- the explicit D7 requirement 'pipe-busy-then-free with paused Tokio time on the Windows lane' has no test at all, not merely an unexecuted-on-Windows-CI fixture (this is a coverage gap, not the AY.7-deferred-execution pattern); (3) no cancellation fixture proves the no-survivor guarantee for connect/write/read stages specifically (only permit-wait cancellation is proven; the existing stalled-read test aborts the *server* task via task.abort(), which does not exercise or prove client-side drop/cancel semantics during an in-flight connect, write, or read). Scoping for the next fix round: add cfg(windows) coverage for connect_named_pipe's busy-retry-then-succeed path under start_paused=true, add stalled-connect/stalled-write fixture scenarios, and add explicit drop-mid-call cancellation tests for the connect/write/read stages proving stream and any retry timer are released." .

<urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AY8-QA-002/ay8-4>
  a triage:Occurrence ;
  triage:file "crates/atm-herdr/tests/support/fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs" ;
  triage:line 158 ;
  triage:snippet "let read = self.server.read(&mut byte).await?;" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:closed false ;
  triage:branch "feature/ay8-herdr-socket-transport" ;
  triage:headSha "6ac58fe44" ;
  triage:occursIn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/ay8-6ac58fe44> ;
  triage:note "quality-mgr qa-pr1280-r5, ruling per explicit non-negotiable directive: cipher's FIX-AY8-R4-20260907 claims the D7 matrix now covers bounded stalled connect/write/read, cancellation, and a Windows named-pipe round-trip with ERROR_PIPE_BUSY retry, with the fixture compiling on both Unix and Windows. Source-level, the stalled-connect/write, cancellation, and pipe-busy-retry unit tests in crates/atm-herdr/src/transport_socket.rs (stalled_connect_honors_the_absolute_deadline, stalled_write_honors_the_absolute_deadline, cancelling_connect_write_and_read_futures_is_clean, named_pipe_busy_retry_is_covered_with_a_bounded_deadline) are real, deterministic (tokio::test(start_paused=true), no wall-clock sleeps) and genuinely close 3 of the 4 remaining r4 gaps -- this is real progress, not a rubber stamp. HOWEVER the claimed Windows named-pipe round-trip does not hold: live CI at this exact pin (gh run list --branch feature/ay8-herdr-socket-transport, run 34089817741, job 101641633365, Test (windows-latest)) FAILS TO COMPILE -- rustc E0599 'no method named `read` found for struct `NamedPipeServer`' at fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs:158, because `use tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;` is missing from that file's imports (AsyncWriteExt appears to be in scope given no write-side error, but AsyncReadExt is not). This means the Windows named-pipe path was never actually validated on the windows-latest toolchain before being reported fixed -- the whole windows-gated portion of the D7 matrix (round-trip test plus the pipe-busy-retry test, both of which live in the same file/module and therefore share this same compile failure on Windows) is unverified dead-on-arrival at this pin. Finding stays open (blocking). Scoping for the next fix round: add `use tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;` (or equivalent) to fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs so the cfg(windows) NamedPipeServer read path compiles, then re-verify the Windows Test-matrix leg goes green at the new pin before claiming this scenario closed." .

<urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AY8-QA-002/ay8-5>
  a triage:Occurrence ;
  triage:file "crates/atm-herdr/tests/support/fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs" ;
  triage:line 184 ;
  triage:snippet "use tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:closed true ;
  triage:branch "feature/ay8-herdr-socket-transport" ;
  triage:headSha "6102354a5" ;
  triage:occursIn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/ay8-6102354a5> ;
  triage:note "quality-mgr qa-pr1280-r6: cipher's fix adds the missing `use tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;` import to fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs's Windows cfg(windows) module, restoring compilation of the NamedPipeServer read path. Verified this is not merely a compile fix: live CI at pin 6102354a5bfadcc6be8f40226671fece03191217, run 34091661579, job 101647017086 (Test (windows-latest)) is green, and its log shows `test windows_named_pipe_fixture_round_trips_in_the_platform_lane ... ok` and `test named_pipe_busy_retry_is_covered_with_a_bounded_deadline ... ok` actually executing and passing (not merely compiling). This closes the last remaining r5 gap for this finding -- the Windows-gated portion of the D7 matrix is now compiled, executed, and green in CI." .

<urn:atm:triage:resolution/AY8-QA-002/1>
  a triage:Resolution ;
  triage:resolvedBy "quality-mgr" ;
  triage:resolvedAt "2026-09-07T07:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:fixCommit "6102354a5bfadcc6be8f40226671fece03191217" ;
  triage:fixBranch "feature/ay8-herdr-socket-transport" ;
  triage:verifiedInRound "qa-pr1280-r6" ;
  triage:resolutionNote "Missing `use tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;` import added to fake_herdr_socket/mod.rs, fixing the E0599 compile failure on the Windows cfg(windows) NamedPipeServer read path identified in r5 at run 34089817741/job 101641633365. Confirmed on live CI at 6102354a5, run 34091661579: Test (windows-latest) job 101647017086 is green, with the job log showing windows_named_pipe_fixture_round_trips_in_the_platform_lane and named_pipe_busy_retry_is_covered_with_a_bounded_deadline both actually executing and passing, not merely compiling. All four D7 sub-requirements this finding tracked (stalled connect/write, cancellation, pipe-busy-retry, Windows compile+execute) are now closed across r5/r6." .

